      commonMistakes: {
        id: 'common-mistakes',
        title: 'Common Mistakes When Evaluating Ollama',
        content: 'Most confusion about Ollama comes from conflating the free local software with the separate paid cloud product, or expecting features (like built-in RAG) it does not include by design.',
        subsections: [
          {
            title: 'Mistake 1: Assuming the cloud tier is required to use Ollama',
            content: 'Ollama\'s CLI, local API, and desktop app are free, MIT-licensed, and run entirely on your own hardware with no account. Ollama Inc.\'s paid cloud tier (from $20/month) is a separate, optional product for running larger models on hosted infrastructure — it is never required for local use.',
          },
          {
            title: 'Mistake 2: Expecting built-in document chat (RAG)',
            content: 'Ollama itself is an inference engine and API, not a full chat application with document upload. To chat with your own documents, pair Ollama with a separate client such as Open WebUI or AnythingLLM that adds a RAG layer on top of Ollama\'s API.',
          },
          {
            title: 'Mistake 3: Confusing Ollama\'s own model names with the original upstream model release',
            content: 'Ollama\'s library uses its own tags (e.g., a specific quantization or fine-tune under a familiar model family name). Always check a model\'s library page on [ollama.com/library](https://ollama.com/library) for its exact parameter count, quantization, and source, rather than assuming it matches every detail of the original model release.',
          },
          {
            title: 'Mistake 4: Assuming stale, third-party star counts',
            content: 'GitHub star counts for a fast-growing project like Ollama go stale quickly. This review verified 186,849 stars directly via the [GitHub API](https://api.github.com/repos/ollama/ollama) on November 13, 2026 — re-check that endpoint yourself for a current figure rather than relying on any cached number, including this one.',
          },
        ],
      },
      faq: {
        id: 'faq',
        title: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
        faqs: [
          { q: 'What is Ollama?', a: 'Ollama ([ollama.com](https://ollama.com), source at [github.com/ollama/ollama](https://github.com/ollama/ollama)) is a free, open-source command-line tool, local API, and desktop app for downloading and running open-weight AI models on your own computer through a built-in llama.cpp-based engine (plus its own MLX engine on Apple Silicon).' },
          { q: 'Is Ollama free?', a: 'Yes. The core software — CLI, local API, and desktop app — is MIT-licensed and free, with no feature gated behind payment. Ollama Inc. separately sells an optional paid cloud tier (from $20/month) for hosted inference on its own servers; that cloud product is not required to use the free local software.' },
          { q: 'Is Ollama open source? What license does it use?', a: 'Yes. Ollama\'s [GitHub LICENSE file](https://github.com/ollama/ollama/blob/main/LICENSE) is the standard MIT License, one of the most permissive open-source licenses, with no copyleft obligation.' },
          { q: 'What platforms does Ollama support?', a: 'macOS (14 Sonoma or later), Windows, and Linux, per the [official download page](https://ollama.com/download). It is also distributed as a Docker image (`ollama/ollama`) and through package managers including Homebrew, Pacman, Nix, and Guix.' },
          { q: 'Does Ollama have a graphical interface, or is it CLI-only?', a: 'Both. Ollama started as a command-line tool and still centers on the CLI, but Ollama Inc. released an official desktop app for macOS and Windows in July 2025, adding a graphical chat interface alongside the terminal workflow.' },
          { q: 'Does Ollama have an OpenAI-compatible API?', a: 'Yes. Alongside its native REST API at `http://localhost:11434`, Ollama exposes an OpenAI-compatible endpoint at `http://localhost:11434/v1`, covering `/v1/chat/completions`, `/v1/completions`, `/v1/models`, and `/v1/embeddings`.' },
          { q: 'What is a Modelfile?', a: 'A Modelfile is a text configuration file used to build a custom Ollama model. It supports `FROM` (base model, required), `PARAMETER`, `TEMPLATE`, `SYSTEM`, `ADAPTER`, `LICENSE`, and `MESSAGE` instructions, and is built into a runnable model with `ollama create <name> -f Modelfile`.' },
          { q: 'How many GitHub stars does Ollama have?', a: 'Ollama\'s repository ([github.com/ollama/ollama](https://github.com/ollama/ollama)) showed 186,849 stars as of November 13, 2026, verified directly via the GitHub API. Check the repository directly for a current count, since it changes daily.' },
          { q: 'Who develops Ollama?', a: 'Ollama Inc., a company based in Palo Alto, California, founded in 2023 by Jeffrey Morgan and Michael Chiang, who previously built [Kitematic](https://github.com/docker/kitematic), a Docker GUI acquired by Docker in 2015.' },
          { q: 'Does Ollama include Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) or document chat?', a: 'Not built in. Ollama provides the inference engine and API; document-chat and RAG functionality typically come from a separate client layered on top, such as Open WebUI or AnythingLLM, both of which can connect to Ollama\'s API.' },
          { q: 'Does Ollama support MLX on Apple Silicon?', a: 'Yes. Ollama announced an MLX-based inference engine for Apple Silicon in preview on March 30, 2026, and later posts describe updates such as NVFP4 support. MLX-format models use tags like `gemma4:12b-mlx`, while GGUF models still run through the llama.cpp-based backend.' },
          { q: 'Can Ollama generate speech?', a: 'Yes, text-to-speech. Since October 24, 2026 Ollama can run Phonon (`ollama run phonon "Hello"`) and serve it through an OpenAI-compatible `/v1/audio/speech` endpoint. At announcement it ran on Apple Silicon Macs only, with Windows and Linux listed as coming soon.' },
          { q: 'What does `ollama launch` do?', a: 'It sets up a coding tool — such as Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, or Droid — to use an Ollama model, local or cloud, without manual environment variables or config files. For example, `ollama launch claude --model my-model` starts Claude Code against that model.' },
          { q: 'How do I run parallel requests in Ollama?', a: 'Set `OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL` to the number of requests each loaded model should handle at once (default 1) and `OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS` to cap loaded models. Memory use grows with parallelism multiplied by context length, and requests beyond `OLLAMA_MAX_QUEUE` (default 512) get an HTTP 503 response.' },
        ],
      },
